1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,785
On the evening of May 6th, 1937, a forty-one-year-old woman named Matilde Doehner was
sitting in the dining room of the airship Hindenburg with her three children.

2
00:00:03,785 --> 00:00:07,570
In twelve minutes, the air outside the
windows would catch fire.

3
00:00:07,570 --> 00:00:11,356
In thirty-four seconds after that, she would have to
choose which of her children she could save.

4
00:00:11,356 --> 00:00:15,141
What you are about to see has been recreated from period photographs, the Doehner family's
own surviving accounts, and the official Lakehurst investigation records.

5
00:00:15,141 --> 00:00:18,926
Three days earlier, the family had
boarded in Frankfurt, Germany.

6
00:00:18,926 --> 00:00:22,711
They were going home.

7
00:00:22,711 --> 00:00:26,496
Home was Mexico City.

8
00:00:26,496 --> 00:00:30,282
Hermann Doehner, Matilde's husband, was forty-nine. He had moved to
Mexico from Germany when he was twenty-one and never left.

9
00:00:30,282 --> 00:00:34,067
He ran the country's largest German
pharmaceutical company.

10
00:00:34,067 --> 00:00:37,852
The family had spent three months in
Germany on a long holiday.

11
00:00:37,852 --> 00:00:41,637
Now they were returning by airship, then by
train south to Mexico City.

12
00:00:41,637 --> 00:00:45,422
The tickets cost seven hundred US dollars per person. About
fifteen thousand dollars today, in 2026 money.

13
00:00:45,422 --> 00:00:49,208
Five tickets. Irene was fourteen. Walter
was ten. Werner was eight.

14
00:00:49,208 --> 00:00:52,993
For three days, the Hindenburg moved across the Atlantic at
one hundred and twenty kilometres an hour.

15
00:00:52,993 --> 00:00:56,778
Slow, by modern standards. The whole
crossing was a single long meal.

16
00:00:56,778 --> 00:01:00,563
The dining room smelled of polished wood
and faintly of engine oil.

17
00:01:00,563 --> 00:01:04,349
The carpet underfoot was so deep you could
not hear your own footsteps.

18
00:01:04,349 --> 00:01:08,134
Through the observation windows, you could see whitecaps
on the ocean four thousand feet below,

19
00:01:08,134 --> 00:01:11,919
but the airship moved so smoothly that the
whitecaps looked like still photographs.

20
00:01:11,919 --> 00:01:15,704
Werner spent the mornings at the windows.

21
00:01:15,704 --> 00:01:19,489
Walter played cards in the lounge.

22
00:01:19,489 --> 00:01:23,275
Irene read.

23
00:01:23,275 --> 00:01:27,060
Hermann had brought a movie camera. He
filmed the family at meals.

24
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:30,845
He filmed the children at the
observation windows.

25
00:01:30,845 --> 00:01:34,630
He filmed the Atlantic from above.

26
00:01:34,630 --> 00:01:38,415
Every evening he carried the camera back to his
cabin and stored it carefully.

27
00:01:38,415 --> 00:01:42,201
He was filming the trip for his family in
Mexico to see when they got home.

28
00:01:42,201 --> 00:01:45,986
The morning of May 6th, the Hindenburg passed
over Boston, hours behind schedule.

29
00:01:45,986 --> 00:01:49,771
By early afternoon it was over New Jersey.

30
00:01:49,771 --> 00:01:53,556
But the weather at Lakehurst had turned.
Thunderstorms had grounded the airfield.

31
00:01:53,556 --> 00:01:57,341
Captain Max Pruss circled the New Jersey coast for
hours, waiting for the storms to pass.

32
00:01:57,341 --> 00:02:01,127
The family ate lunch. Then they ate an early
dinner. Then they waited.

33
00:02:01,127 --> 00:02:04,912
By half past six, the storms had cleared.

34
00:02:04,912 --> 00:02:08,697
The ship turned inland toward the
mooring mast at Lakehurst.

35
00:02:08,697 --> 00:02:12,482
A small crowd of relatives and journalists had been
waiting on the ground since morning.

36
00:02:12,482 --> 00:02:16,267
Hermann took out his movie camera.
He started filming.

37
00:02:16,267 --> 00:02:20,053
At seven twenty-one PM, the Hindenburg was two
hundred feet above the field.

38
00:02:20,053 --> 00:02:23,838
The mooring lines were dropped to
the ground crew.

39
00:02:23,838 --> 00:02:27,623
The descent toward the mast began.

40
00:02:27,623 --> 00:02:31,408
Hermann was still at the window. He had been filming
the descent for perhaps three minutes

41
00:02:31,408 --> 00:02:35,193
when he lowered the camera and turned to walk back
toward his cabin to put it away.

42
00:02:35,193 --> 00:02:38,979
At seven twenty-five PM, something began to
flicker at the top of the tail.

43
00:02:38,979 --> 00:02:42,764
From the ground it looked at first like
a mushroom of light.

44
00:02:42,764 --> 00:02:46,549
Within four seconds, it had spread the
length of the airship.

45
00:02:46,549 --> 00:02:50,334
Inside the dining room, the
windows turned orange.

46
00:02:50,334 --> 00:02:54,120
The light came from everywhere at once.

47
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:57,905
The Hindenburg burned for
thirty-four seconds.

48
00:02:57,905 --> 00:03:01,690
The airship's hull was on the
ground within seconds.

49
00:03:01,690 --> 00:03:05,475
But the dining room, on the portside mid-ship, was
still fifteen to twenty feet up, tilted,

50
00:03:05,475 --> 00:03:09,260
with the observation windows
facing the open field.

51
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:13,046
Matilde had three children with her
in the dining room.

52
00:03:13,046 --> 00:03:16,831
Hermann was somewhere on the other side
of the burning airship.

53
00:03:16,831 --> 00:03:20,616
She took Walter first. He was ten.

54
00:03:20,616 --> 00:03:24,401
She lifted him to the window and threw him out. He
bounced off the window frame on the way down.

55
00:03:24,401 --> 00:03:28,186
She had to throw him out a second time.

56
00:03:28,186 --> 00:03:31,972
Then Werner. He was eight.

57
00:03:31,972 --> 00:03:35,757
She turned back for Irene. Irene was
fourteen. She was too heavy.

58
00:03:35,757 --> 00:03:39,542
Matilde had to make a choice she
had no time to make.

59
00:03:39,542 --> 00:03:43,327
She climbed onto the window frame
herself, alone. She jumped.

60
00:03:43,327 --> 00:03:47,112
She broke her pelvis on landing.

61
00:03:47,112 --> 00:03:50,898
Ground crew pulled Irene from
the wreckage.

62
00:03:50,898 --> 00:03:54,683
She was alive. She was severely burned.

63
00:03:54,683 --> 00:03:58,468
They drove her to Point Pleasant
Hospital that night.

64
00:03:58,468 --> 00:04:02,253
A nurse fainted at the sight of her
injuries when she was brought in.

65
00:04:02,253 --> 00:04:06,038
Irene died early the next morning.

66
00:04:06,038 --> 00:04:09,824
The next day's newspapers listed
Hermann Doehner as missing.

67
00:04:09,824 --> 00:04:13,609
His body was found later that day in the
wreckage of the dining room area.

68
00:04:13,609 --> 00:04:17,394
He was identified by his wedding ring.

69
00:04:17,394 --> 00:04:21,179
Matilde, Walter, and Werner survived.

70
00:04:21,179 --> 00:04:24,964
The two boys had burns on their
hands, faces, and legs.

71
00:04:24,964 --> 00:04:28,750
Werner would be in the hospital
for three months.

72
00:04:28,750 --> 00:04:32,535
The Hindenburg disaster killed thirteen of the thirty-six
passengers and twenty-two of the sixty-one crew.

73
00:04:32,535 --> 00:04:36,320
The Doehner family lost two of five.

74
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:40,105
Matilde lived until 1972.

75
00:04:40,105 --> 00:04:43,890
She was buried in Mexico City beside her
husband and her daughter.

76
00:04:43,890 --> 00:04:47,676
Walter became a chemist.

77
00:04:47,676 --> 00:04:51,461
He died in the 1950s.

78
00:04:51,461 --> 00:04:55,246
Werner became an electrical engineer.

79
00:04:55,246 --> 00:04:59,031
He lived in Mexico, then Colorado,
then New Hampshire.

80
00:04:59,031 --> 00:05:02,817
He almost never spoke about what happened.

81
00:05:02,817 --> 00:05:06,602
When he died in 2019, at age 90, he was the last living
person who had been on the Hindenburg.

82
00:05:06,602 --> 00:05:10,387
The film in Hermann's camera was
never recovered.
